# WP3-H4 — inline application logic moved into testable modules

Run 2026-09-23, darwin 25.6.0, Node v22.23.1, pnpm 9.15.0.
Branch `program/developer-trust`, uncommitted working tree, HEAD `b4615721`.

WP3 requirement being evidenced (plan §WP3 bullet 4, second half):
  "Inline application logic moved into testable modules."

**Status: PARTIALLY DONE — and the plan's stated mechanism turned out not to exist.** A narrower
mechanism does, and it is implemented, behaviourally tested, and verified in a real browser. What
cannot move is stated below with the reason, and what was deliberately held is carried as **B14**.

## 1. The premise, overturned

The item was recorded as "`index.html` holds ~2,150 lines of inline `<script>` logic". Read at the
source, that is not what the asset contains:

| | Measured |
| --- | --- |
| `index.html` total | 2,213 lines |
| Of which the inline block | **1,527 lines** |
| Its opening tag | `<script type="text/x-dc" data-dc-script data-props="…">` |

`type="text/x-dc"` is not JavaScript to the browser — it is a **DC component source**, a payload the
runtime reads out of the DOM. `support.js` locates it with

    doc.querySelector("script[data-dc-script]")        // support.js:27, :45

and its own failure path at `support.js:1372` reports `"has no <x-dc> block — not a Design Component."`

So the register's gate condition (a) — *"move logic to a module loaded by `<script src>`"* — is
**structurally impossible** for this payload. `<script src>` content is never in the document, so the
query returns null and the canvas does not boot. The mechanism is not hard; it is unavailable.

`support.js` is not a way out either: line 1 is

    // GENERATED from dc-runtime/src/*.ts — do not edit. Rebuild with `cd dc-runtime && bun run build`.

and **no `dc-runtime/` tree exists in this repository** (the same provenance finding as H1). It is a
checked-in build artifact with its generator absent: unbuildable, unmodifiable.

## 2. What the repository had already done, and extending it

H4's pattern was already present, just not named as such. `graph-model.js` had previously been
extracted out of that same component and is loaded as

    <script src="./graph-model.js">          // a classic script; exposes root.KCGraphModel

and driven behaviourally by `graph-model.test.ts` via `runInNewContext` against a fake `globalThis` —
genuine behavioural testing, already in place.

This increment extended that in the same direction. The selection rule was **purity, not
convenience**: a method whose body reads neither component state nor the DOM is a function of its
arguments alone and does not belong in an asset that can only be asserted by string match.

    function hex(c, a)                      #rrggbb + alpha -> rgba(); non-`#` passes through
    function esc(s)                         escape dynamic text before splicing into innerHTML
    function ellipsize(ctx, text, maxWidth) binary search for the longest prefix that fits, + '…'
    function rr(ctx, x, y, w, h, r)         rounded-rect subpath; emits path commands only

None of the four uses `this` in any form.

## 3. The move, and why it is behaviour-preserving by construction

**Deleted** the four definitions from the component (`index.html`, old lines 1455–1461) and **rewrote
34 call sites** to go through the module:

| helper | call sites rewritten |
| --- | --- |
| `hex` | 18 |
| `ellipsize` | 7 |
| `rr` | 6 |
| `esc` | 3 |
| **total** | **34** |

Zero leftovers in any form — not `this.hex(`, not `.hex(`, not a bare call. Byte delta reconciles:
187,727 → 186,946 = **Δ781**, against the deleted definitions plus 34 × 8 added bytes (`KCGraphModel.`
is 13 characters where `this.` is 5, so +8 per site).

One deliberate source change inside a moved body, and it is worth naming because "behaviour-preserving"
is a claim that must survive its own exceptions: `ellipsize` originally **reassigned its parameter**
(`text = String(text || '')`). Biome's `noParameterAssign` is on, so the moved version introduces a
local (`const s = String(text || '')`) and reads it. Same value, same control flow, no reassignment —
the exception is to the linter, not to the behaviour.

Because every moved body is `this`-free and logic-identical, **the only way this change can alter
rendering is a missed call site.** So the verification targets exactly that failure and nothing else:

    leftover `this.<helper>(` in any form       0
    helper definitions remaining in the asset   none
    component still parses as a class           new Function('class DCLogic{}\n' + block) -> PASS
    all eight KCGraphModel exports present      yes
    smoke: hex('#5b8cff',0.5)                   rgba(91,140,255,0.5)
    smoke: esc('<T>')                           &lt;T&gt;

`KCGraphModel` was already in scope at every site — the asset calls `KCGraphModel.buildIndexes`
(:821), `.clusterProjection` (:1087), `.searchProjection` (:1093), `.edgeIndexesForNodeIds` (:1104) —
so no load-order change was introduced.

## 4. The string pins replaced by behaviour (gate condition (b))

Twelve tests added to `packages/ui/src/graph-model.test.ts`, driving the real module loaded from disk.
The two that carry real weight:

  - **`ellipsize` maximality is a property, not an example.** The binary search has two ways to be
    wrong — overrun the budget, or stop early and render a label narrower than the room it was given —
    and an example test pins neither. Over budgets 7…308 step 7 (7 px per character), for every output:
    width ≤ budget, and when the output ends in `…`, **one more character would have exceeded it**.
  - **`rr` emits an exact 7-call sequence**, `[beginPath, moveTo(15,20), arcTo ×4, closePath]` for
    `rr(ctx,10,20,100,60,5)`, and a second test pins that it never fills or strokes — the caller chooses
    fill vs stroke, so a fill in here would change every call site at once.

Plus `hex` (channel extraction, non-`#` passthrough, alpha 0 not read as "absent"), and `esc` (the
four escapes, ampersand-first so `&lt;` becomes `&amp;lt;` rather than double-decoding, `null` →
`''` rather than the text "null").

## 5. The Gate-0 law was extended to what is SERVED — the prerequisite for any extraction

Before moving anything, the vocabulary law in `web-assets.test.ts` was widened from `index.html` to

    const SERVED_ASSETS = ['index.html', 'graph-model.js', 'support.js'] as const;

A law that covers only one filename is a law code can be **walked out of**: a banned word in
`graph-model.js` reaches the user exactly as one in the markup would. Widening the law first is what
makes this extraction a coverage *increase* rather than a scope shrink. The assertions are unchanged;
only the set of files they run over is.

Non-vacuity was demonstrated, not assumed — an injected string-literal banned word is caught in **all
three** assets. And the strip is shown to be load-bearing rather than incidentally green:

    index.html       raw=["trust"]   stripped=[]
    graph-model.js   raw=[]          stripped=[]
    support.js       raw=[]          stripped=[]

`index.html`'s raw hit is a real code comment (line 767, `// Trust these over re-deriving …`) — exactly
what the comment-strip exists to exclude.

**The law's honest limit, measured rather than asserted.** The line-based `//` rule eats the rest of a
line when a `//` appears inside a string literal:

    var u = "https://x"; var t = "trust";     ->  no hit      (the banned word is now invisible)
    var u = "http:x";    var t = "trust";     ->  ["trust"]   (control: only the `//` was removed)

The error is therefore always **under**-reporting — never a false alarm. It is **dormant today**: zero
lines in any of the three served assets carry both an inline `//` and a banned word, so the guarantee
holds for this tree even though the rule cannot be trusted to hold for a future one. This is stated in
the helper's own docblock, because it bounds the guarantee the test makes.

## 6. What did NOT move, and why

The memory-panel vocabulary helpers (`memAxis`, `memChip`) are pure and would be easy extractions.
They were **deliberately left in the asset**. `memAxis` contains the decisive find of this increment:

    memAxis(v){ const raw=v===void 0?'':String(v);
      if(raw===String.fromCharCode(99,97,110,100,105,100,97,116,101)) return 'staged';
      if(raw===String.fromCharCode(116,114,117,115,116)) return 'standing';
      return raw; }

Those two char-code sequences decode to the **two words the Gate-0 law exists to keep out**. They are
the **only two `String.fromCharCode` uses in the entire served asset set** (`index.html` 2,
`graph-model.js` 0, `support.js` 0). The rendered text is compliant — `'staged'` / `'standing'` — but
the construction is un-auditable by inspection, and `memAxis` is the **sole** translation boundary
between the backend's stored enum values and the user-facing vocabulary (called at `index.html:1813`,
`:1816`, `:2172`, `:2174`), with a `return raw` fallthrough.

Note what this does and does not mean. It is **not** a scope problem: §5 already extended the law over
every served file, so there is no longer a file to launder a banned word into. The surviving hazard is
sharper and is **unchanged by scope**: a law that matches literals structurally cannot see a word that
is never written as one. Extracting `memAxis` would not fix that, and would have the side effect of
splitting a pair of sibling helpers so one is testable and the other is not.

Deciding this means editing the Gate-0 law to admit the very two words it exists to keep out (a
de-obfuscation puts `'candidate'`/`'trust'` back into the asset as comparison operands, which the
vocabulary law cannot distinguish from user-facing text). That is a policy change to a law, so it is
carried as **B14** with a recommendation and not applied here.

## 7. Verification

    $ corepack pnpm@9.15.0 -C packages/ui exec vitest run
    ✓ src/web-assets.test.ts (13 tests)
    ✓ src/graph-model.test.ts (16 tests)
    ✓ src/viz.test.ts (9 tests)
    Test Files  3 passed (3)      Tests  38 passed (38)

    $ corepack pnpm@9.15.0 verify:browser         # real browser, real isolated backend
    13 passed (18.5s)

    $ npx biome check packages/ui/web/graph-model.js packages/ui/src/graph-model.test.ts packages/ui/src/web-assets.test.ts
    Checked 3 files in 4ms. No fixes applied.

The browser suite matters here specifically: the extraction touches the **drawing path**, and
`web-assets.test.ts` cannot see a rendering regression — it asserts strings. 13 acceptance tests drive
the served asset in Chromium against a real backend, which is the independent check that the moved
helpers still render.

## 8. What this increment does NOT license

- **The component payload is not testable and this did not make it so.** 1,527 lines of component
  source still sit in the served document, outside every check, and cannot move (§1). Four pure helpers
  and their 34 call sites were removed from it — that is the whole claim.
- **A behaviour-preserving argument is not a proof.** It rests on `this`-freedom plus a zero-leftover
  call-site count; a call site the count cannot see would break rendering silently, and the browser
  suite is what would have to catch it. It did not, but an absence of failure is not a demonstration of
  impossibility.
- **The vocabulary law still cannot see an obfuscated word** (§6), and the extended scope does not
  change that.
- **The strip under-reports** on a line carrying both an inline `//` and a banned word (§5). Dormant
  today; not prevented tomorrow.
- **The helpers are covered; the memory panel's logic is not.** H4 remains partial — see **B14**.
